[{:db/id "4e3b10f7-1ab2-5dd0-bdc2-2c023a53bdc4" :answer/id #uuid "4e3b10f7-1ab2-5dd0-bdc2-2c023a53bdc4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,1,2⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "65a60baf-69b2-5fe6-90a1-c8f938f6de10" :answer/id #uuid "65a60baf-69b2-5fe6-90a1-c8f938f6de10" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,-2,1⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "10ba9c0a-1858-5d2b-ae82-2126392ec58e" :answer/id #uuid "10ba9c0a-1858-5d2b-ae82-2126392ec58e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,1,-2⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "f2e01698-f591-5f18-9466-8532bda73ad2" :answer/id #uuid "f2e01698-f591-5f18-9466-8532bda73ad2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,0,0⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "72d61d09-f7e2-5f33-b750-f198d1889040" :answer/id #uuid "72d61d09-f7e2-5f33-b750-f198d1889040" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,-2,-1⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "777c8854-9f2f-5bcb-b4b6-3afe85450d1d" :answer-field/id #uuid "777c8854-9f2f-5bcb-b4b6-3afe85450d1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["4e3b10f7-1ab2-5dd0-bdc2-2c023a53bdc4" "65a60baf-69b2-5fe6-90a1-c8f938f6de10" "10ba9c0a-1858-5d2b-ae82-2126392ec58e" "f2e01698-f591-5f18-9466-8532bda73ad2" "72d61d09-f7e2-5f33-b750-f198d1889040"] :answer-field/correct "10ba9c0a-1858-5d2b-ae82-2126392ec58e" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "48d0933d-38f4-5fca-ac17-2cb0bd7b696b" :question/id #uuid "48d0933d-38f4-5fca-ac17-2cb0bd7b696b" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=3.$ Given that the point $P(3,3,-3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=-2$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(3,3).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(3,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(3,3) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(3,3) & ={f}_{y}(3,3)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =(-2)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =6 - 2 y \\\\ z & =3 - 2 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(3,3,-3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{x=3 \\\\ z=3 - 2 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=3,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=3 - 2 y$ (or $- 2 y - z + 3=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,-2,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,-2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j - 2 k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,-2⟩.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["777c8854-9f2f-5bcb-b4b6-3afe85450d1d"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "b0c1d413-8151-5d44-94fe-2a943194fc87" :answer/id #uuid "b0c1d413-8151-5d44-94fe-2a943194fc87" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,0,0⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "c164c3ce-a97a-5789-ae86-81689ef5e741" :answer/id #uuid "c164c3ce-a97a-5789-ae86-81689ef5e741" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,1,8⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "7c4acddb-f393-5ef8-8512-f2734c2b7b87" :answer/id #uuid "7c4acddb-f393-5ef8-8512-f2734c2b7b87" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,8,1⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "37dd67a6-9302-52ce-a4eb-b458c3c38495" :answer/id #uuid "37dd67a6-9302-52ce-a4eb-b458c3c38495" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,1,-8⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "87120362-d259-56d3-979a-9bd2346dda46" :answer/id #uuid "87120362-d259-56d3-979a-9bd2346dda46" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,8,-1⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "a4340bac-2429-5c45-a387-f044a1812b92" :answer-field/id #uuid "a4340bac-2429-5c45-a387-f044a1812b92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["b0c1d413-8151-5d44-94fe-2a943194fc87" "c164c3ce-a97a-5789-ae86-81689ef5e741" "7c4acddb-f393-5ef8-8512-f2734c2b7b87" "37dd67a6-9302-52ce-a4eb-b458c3c38495" "87120362-d259-56d3-979a-9bd2346dda46"] :answer-field/correct "c164c3ce-a97a-5789-ae86-81689ef5e741" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "76fc75e5-bc34-5a11-827a-718027bfa6d6" :question/id #uuid "76fc75e5-bc34-5a11-827a-718027bfa6d6" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,2,-3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=8$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,2) & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,2) & ={f}_{y}(1,2)(y-(2)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =(8)(y-(2)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =8 y - 16 \\\\ z & =8 y - 19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,2,-3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{x=1 \\\\ z=8 y - 19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=8 y - 19$ (or $8 y - z - 19=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,8,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,8,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j + 8 k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,8⟩.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["a4340bac-2429-5c45-a387-f044a1812b92"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "0c3e1e95-6abb-5b2d-b333-2c9928f10fe8" :answer/id #uuid "0c3e1e95-6abb-5b2d-b333-2c9928f10fe8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨3,0,1⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "7f9ce72c-9b00-57d7-ab95-a6ee0fc09ad5" :answer/id #uuid "7f9ce72c-9b00-57d7-ab95-a6ee0fc09ad5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,0,3⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "5e3dd612-7efb-572c-98e3-50b684d7a1f5" :answer/id #uuid "5e3dd612-7efb-572c-98e3-50b684d7a1f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨3,0,-1⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "b298ee27-0c63-5917-9d3e-0528f700c1b3" :answer/id #uuid "b298ee27-0c63-5917-9d3e-0528f700c1b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,1,0⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "ac5fd2fc-a327-5afc-af0f-ecca1c720272" :answer/id #uuid "ac5fd2fc-a327-5afc-af0f-ecca1c720272" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,0,-3⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "7f426c50-eced-5e9c-a349-73aa145929b3" :answer-field/id #uuid "7f426c50-eced-5e9c-a349-73aa145929b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["0c3e1e95-6abb-5b2d-b333-2c9928f10fe8" "7f9ce72c-9b00-57d7-ab95-a6ee0fc09ad5" "5e3dd612-7efb-572c-98e3-50b684d7a1f5" "b298ee27-0c63-5917-9d3e-0528f700c1b3" "ac5fd2fc-a327-5afc-af0f-ecca1c720272"] :answer-field/correct "7f9ce72c-9b00-57d7-ab95-a6ee0fc09ad5" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "24dbd77f-2590-58ef-821c-6bd1e8a62bdc" :question/id #uuid "24dbd77f-2590-58ef-821c-6bd1e8a62bdc" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=4.$ Given that the point $P(-2,4,-4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=3$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=4.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-2,4).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-2,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-2,4) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,4) & ={f}_{x}(-2,4)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(-4) & =(3)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(-4) & =3 x + 6 \\\\ z & =3 x + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,4,-4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{y=4 \\\\ z=3 x + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=4,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=3 x + 2$ (or $3 x - z + 2=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨3,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =- i - 3 k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-3⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(⟨-1,0,-3⟩)=⟨1,0,3⟩$ is also parallel to it." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["7f426c50-eced-5e9c-a349-73aa145929b3"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "13415e5b-d7ce-5839-ac8a-9fdda0db113d" :answer/id #uuid "13415e5b-d7ce-5839-ac8a-9fdda0db113d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,0,2⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "58a13d5a-69b9-5fed-8522-be2e66a2c2f1" :answer/id #uuid "58a13d5a-69b9-5fed-8522-be2e66a2c2f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨-2,0,-1⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "4abd2572-3ff6-52e7-b470-d37775dc5e9a" :answer/id #uuid "4abd2572-3ff6-52e7-b470-d37775dc5e9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,0,-2⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "88e51073-51d9-5a66-b57f-a46e981e04ac" :answer/id #uuid "88e51073-51d9-5a66-b57f-a46e981e04ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨-2,0,1⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "d6d2fa4a-ce82-5f44-aa09-5b63b8171852" :answer/id #uuid "d6d2fa4a-ce82-5f44-aa09-5b63b8171852" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,1,0⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "ff0d9c98-1039-5b45-b75d-19a75d93883f" :answer-field/id #uuid "ff0d9c98-1039-5b45-b75d-19a75d93883f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["13415e5b-d7ce-5839-ac8a-9fdda0db113d" "58a13d5a-69b9-5fed-8522-be2e66a2c2f1" "4abd2572-3ff6-52e7-b470-d37775dc5e9a" "88e51073-51d9-5a66-b57f-a46e981e04ac" "d6d2fa4a-ce82-5f44-aa09-5b63b8171852"] :answer-field/correct "4abd2572-3ff6-52e7-b470-d37775dc5e9a" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "e5e09c79-be5e-59c8-bad2-508a4b285ff7" :question/id #uuid "e5e09c79-be5e-59c8-bad2-508a4b285ff7" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,2,0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=-2$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-2,2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-2,2) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,2) & ={f}_{x}(-2,2)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(0) & =(-2)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(0) & =- 2 x - 4 \\\\ z & =- 2 x - 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,2,0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{y=2 \\\\ z=- 2 x - 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=- 2 x - 4$ (or $- 2 x - z - 4=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨-2,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨-2,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =- i + 2 k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,2⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(⟨-1,0,2⟩)=⟨1,0,-2⟩$ is also parallel to it." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["ff0d9c98-1039-5b45-b75d-19a75d93883f"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "5a03e80c-0292-50fe-a3ba-354f93996f82" :answer/id #uuid "5a03e80c-0292-50fe-a3ba-354f93996f82" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,2,1⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "49af77ba-23b1-5c02-ae78-e0a7f3dcabf1" :answer/id #uuid "49af77ba-23b1-5c02-ae78-e0a7f3dcabf1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,2,-1⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "63614bc9-8e20-5bf1-8110-d978ebea603a" :answer/id #uuid "63614bc9-8e20-5bf1-8110-d978ebea603a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,0,0⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "ee0f23c0-bab4-5a6d-b985-d5db0527d0d3" :answer/id #uuid "ee0f23c0-bab4-5a6d-b985-d5db0527d0d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,1,-2⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "dfefda43-a9c6-542a-aa55-6d293ada18bf" :answer/id #uuid "dfefda43-a9c6-542a-aa55-6d293ada18bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,1,2⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "c9ff40d2-36af-55fb-8db5-205b757e863c" :answer-field/id #uuid "c9ff40d2-36af-55fb-8db5-205b757e863c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["5a03e80c-0292-50fe-a3ba-354f93996f82" "49af77ba-23b1-5c02-ae78-e0a7f3dcabf1" "63614bc9-8e20-5bf1-8110-d978ebea603a" "ee0f23c0-bab4-5a6d-b985-d5db0527d0d3" "dfefda43-a9c6-542a-aa55-6d293ada18bf"] :answer-field/correct "dfefda43-a9c6-542a-aa55-6d293ada18bf" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "87773f30-7347-58c3-bc15-641532470ff8" :question/id #uuid "87773f30-7347-58c3-bc15-641532470ff8" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-3.$ Given that the point $P(-3,3,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=2$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-3,3).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-3,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-3,3) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-3,3) & ={f}_{y}(-3,3)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(2)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-1) & =2 y - 6 \\\\ z & =2 y - 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-3,3,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{x=-3 \\\\ z=2 y - 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-3,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=2 y - 7$ (or $2 y - z - 7=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,2,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j + 2 k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,2⟩.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["c9ff40d2-36af-55fb-8db5-205b757e863c"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "cbc46a02-9995-525c-8cb6-ea5098fa7457" :answer/id #uuid "cbc46a02-9995-525c-8cb6-ea5098fa7457" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,1,6⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "015bc2bf-13ce-5c4f-8f0c-a4a74231f0c3" :answer/id #uuid "015bc2bf-13ce-5c4f-8f0c-a4a74231f0c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,-6,1⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "9afc3218-bbc9-5554-9630-980dd247d59b" :answer/id #uuid "9afc3218-bbc9-5554-9630-980dd247d59b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,1,-6⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "ff84763e-3842-594a-98b1-79f342d9e445" :answer/id #uuid "ff84763e-3842-594a-98b1-79f342d9e445" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,0,0⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "58d0c333-0126-5eb6-89df-cfb21a2d9ae7" :answer/id #uuid "58d0c333-0126-5eb6-89df-cfb21a2d9ae7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,-6,-1⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "646eda7e-2147-525c-aef1-fd3fda68bebd" :answer-field/id #uuid "646eda7e-2147-525c-aef1-fd3fda68bebd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["cbc46a02-9995-525c-8cb6-ea5098fa7457" "015bc2bf-13ce-5c4f-8f0c-a4a74231f0c3" "9afc3218-bbc9-5554-9630-980dd247d59b" "ff84763e-3842-594a-98b1-79f342d9e445" "58d0c333-0126-5eb6-89df-cfb21a2d9ae7"] :answer-field/correct "9afc3218-bbc9-5554-9630-980dd247d59b" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "322047e2-3976-5636-bcd1-e92ee6b570c4" :question/id #uuid "322047e2-3976-5636-bcd1-e92ee6b570c4" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=3.$ Given that the point $P(3,3,-3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=(-2)y$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(3,3).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(3,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(3,3) & =(-2)(3)=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(3,3) & ={f}_{y}(3,3)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =(-6)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =18 - 6 y \\\\ z & =15 - 6 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(3,3,-3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{x=3 \\\\ z=15 - 6 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=3,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=15 - 6 y$ (or $- 6 y - z + 15=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,-6,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,-6,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j - 6 k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,-6⟩.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["646eda7e-2147-525c-aef1-fd3fda68bebd"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "c5f9d2d0-aacd-5bf3-bbfb-0fba361f5592" :answer/id #uuid "c5f9d2d0-aacd-5bf3-bbfb-0fba361f5592" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,0,0⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "550d8d58-5825-568a-904a-a283edc87093" :answer/id #uuid "550d8d58-5825-568a-904a-a283edc87093" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,1,16⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "0a2d6f59-2293-503c-954b-099ede3ec4ab" :answer/id #uuid "0a2d6f59-2293-503c-954b-099ede3ec4ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,16,1⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "91cf613e-e775-536e-b2b2-d6c5690147d2" :answer/id #uuid "91cf613e-e775-536e-b2b2-d6c5690147d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,1,-16⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "d6acb0ee-9654-5419-9420-f6821a5f49f2" :answer/id #uuid "d6acb0ee-9654-5419-9420-f6821a5f49f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,16,-1⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "35a54cb9-6e14-5105-89d2-938f4cb3495a" :answer-field/id #uuid "35a54cb9-6e14-5105-89d2-938f4cb3495a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["c5f9d2d0-aacd-5bf3-bbfb-0fba361f5592" "550d8d58-5825-568a-904a-a283edc87093" "0a2d6f59-2293-503c-954b-099ede3ec4ab" "91cf613e-e775-536e-b2b2-d6c5690147d2" "d6acb0ee-9654-5419-9420-f6821a5f49f2"] :answer-field/correct "550d8d58-5825-568a-904a-a283edc87093" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "8f308815-8a1f-5bcf-b629-6357db90aa05" :question/id #uuid "8f308815-8a1f-5bcf-b629-6357db90aa05" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,2,-3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=(8)y$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,2) & =(8)(2)=16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,2) & ={f}_{y}(1,2)(y-(2)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =(16)(y-(2)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =16 y - 32 \\\\ z & =16 y - 35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,2,-3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{x=1 \\\\ z=16 y - 35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=16 y - 35$ (or $16 y - z - 35=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,16,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,16,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 16 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j + 16 k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,16⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,16⟩.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["35a54cb9-6e14-5105-89d2-938f4cb3495a"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "e85558f7-5bb9-5e70-8631-2dd61ed3184f" :answer/id #uuid "e85558f7-5bb9-5e70-8631-2dd61ed3184f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨-6,0,1⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "9d66afe9-4f16-5fcb-ad94-4581dad49b13" :answer/id #uuid "9d66afe9-4f16-5fcb-ad94-4581dad49b13" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,0,-6⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "8c44812f-aa8f-5a3b-a2c9-3a9af8c5a229" :answer/id #uuid "8c44812f-aa8f-5a3b-a2c9-3a9af8c5a229" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨-6,0,-1⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "ab7a8a71-8f71-5f9a-b3fd-db2af683847a" :answer/id #uuid "ab7a8a71-8f71-5f9a-b3fd-db2af683847a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,1,0⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "ca4ef977-ff0a-57f1-b6ad-8ad2f3a7abed" :answer/id #uuid "ca4ef977-ff0a-57f1-b6ad-8ad2f3a7abed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,0,6⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "39fe770b-a34b-5bb0-b24e-2cb8ef45a74d" :answer-field/id #uuid "39fe770b-a34b-5bb0-b24e-2cb8ef45a74d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["e85558f7-5bb9-5e70-8631-2dd61ed3184f" "9d66afe9-4f16-5fcb-ad94-4581dad49b13" "8c44812f-aa8f-5a3b-a2c9-3a9af8c5a229" "ab7a8a71-8f71-5f9a-b3fd-db2af683847a" "ca4ef977-ff0a-57f1-b6ad-8ad2f3a7abed"] :answer-field/correct "9d66afe9-4f16-5fcb-ad94-4581dad49b13" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "dd9d8206-b563-5e86-ab9a-f167bb3c1e2b" :question/id #uuid "dd9d8206-b563-5e86-ab9a-f167bb3c1e2b" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=4.$ Given that the point $P(-2,4,-4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=(3)x$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=4.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-2,4).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-2,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-2,4) & =(3)(-2)=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,4) & ={f}_{x}(-2,4)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(-4) & =(-6)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(-4) & =- 6 x - 12 \\\\ z & =- 6 x - 16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,4,-4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{y=4 \\\\ z=- 6 x - 16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=4,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=- 6 x - 16$ (or $- 6 x - z - 16=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨-6,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨-6,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =- i + 6 k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,6⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(⟨-1,0,6⟩)=⟨1,0,-6⟩$ is also parallel to it." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["39fe770b-a34b-5bb0-b24e-2cb8ef45a74d"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "f703c35e-0bb7-5ae6-ac28-2ef517196952" :answer/id #uuid "f703c35e-0bb7-5ae6-ac28-2ef517196952" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,0,-4⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "04fcaa4c-cdc7-55cc-9fbc-bf020baece7a" :answer/id #uuid "04fcaa4c-cdc7-55cc-9fbc-bf020baece7a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨4,0,-1⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "fee29037-72b8-57de-8e34-703bacf1d7c4" :answer/id #uuid "fee29037-72b8-57de-8e34-703bacf1d7c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,0,4⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "3aaf0daf-4db1-56a4-85c0-b9d13bb393d5" :answer/id #uuid "3aaf0daf-4db1-56a4-85c0-b9d13bb393d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨4,0,1⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "89d7a47f-5ffb-5fb7-8773-673d0bdca988" :answer/id #uuid "89d7a47f-5ffb-5fb7-8773-673d0bdca988" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,1,0⟩" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "778574b8-fa99-5187-b7ff-a835f64fafed" :answer-field/id #uuid "778574b8-fa99-5187-b7ff-a835f64fafed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["f703c35e-0bb7-5ae6-ac28-2ef517196952" "04fcaa4c-cdc7-55cc-9fbc-bf020baece7a" "fee29037-72b8-57de-8e34-703bacf1d7c4" "3aaf0daf-4db1-56a4-85c0-b9d13bb393d5" "89d7a47f-5ffb-5fb7-8773-673d0bdca988"] :answer-field/correct "fee29037-72b8-57de-8e34-703bacf1d7c4" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "055263bb-00e6-5ecd-9052-3f0991382dda" :question/id #uuid "055263bb-00e6-5ecd-9052-3f0991382dda" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,2,0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=(-2)x$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-2,2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-2,2) & =(-2)(-2)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,2) & ={f}_{x}(-2,2)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(0) & =(4)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(0) & =4 x + 8 \\\\ z & =4 x + 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,2,0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{y=2 \\\\ z=4 x + 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=4 x + 8$ (or $4 x - z + 8=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨4,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨4,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =- i - 4 k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-4⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(⟨-1,0,-4⟩)=⟨1,0,4⟩$ is also parallel to it." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["778574b8-fa99-5187-b7ff-a835f64fafed"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "f9241366-8977-556e-a838-84ae8f1a7a55" :answer/id #uuid "f9241366-8977-556e-a838-84ae8f1a7a55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,6,1